        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "add per-conversation whatsapp debounce config",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds per-conversation inbound debounce configuration for WhatsApp direct chats and groups.\n\nPractically, this controls how long OpenClaw waits before starting an agent turn after receiving a WhatsApp message from a specific conversation. This is useful when a contact sends multiple messages in a row: OpenClaw can wait a few seconds or minutes, collect the full burst, and then let the agent process the conversation with better context.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe existing WhatsApp debounce setting is too broad for mixed usage.\n\nSome conversations should be processed immediately, while others should wait briefly:\n\n- owner/admin chats often need immediate processing, so they can use `debounceMs: 0`\n- external contacts often send multiple short messages in a row, so they can use a longer debounce, such as `180000`, to wait 3 minutes\n- noisy groups can have their own debounce window\n- wildcard entries can define defaults for all direct chats or all groups\n\nThis lets operators tune batching by conversation without changing the global WhatsApp debounce for every chat.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- supports `debounceMs` in `channels.whatsapp.direct`\n- supports `debounceMs` in `channels.whatsapp.groups`\n- supports wildcard entries like `direct[\"*\"]` and `groups[\"*\"]`\n- specific conversation config wins over wildcard config\n- falls back to the existing global WhatsApp debounce when no conversation override exists\n- `debounceMs: 0` disables debounce for that conversation only\n\n## Evidence\n\nRedacted real WhatsApp setup proof from a local OpenClaw home:\n\nThe run used the PR branch plus real WhatsApp session keys from the local OpenClaw session index. The proof output prints only counts and redacted aliases; it does not include phone numbers, group JIDs, contact names, credentials, or message bodies. It feeds those real direct/group session keys through the same keyed inbound debouncer that the WhatsApp listener calls through `resolveDebounceMs`.\n\n```text\n$ node --import tsx <redacted local proof script>\n   0ms real-session-inventory direct=200 group=3\n   0ms config <redacted-direct-1> debounceMs=0\n   0ms config <redacted-direct-2> debounceMs=120\n   0ms config <redacted-group-1> debounceMs=180\n   0ms flush <redacted-direct-1> items=1 debounceMs=0\n   0ms flush <redacted-direct-1> items=1 debounceMs=0\n 151ms flush <redacted-direct-2> items=2 debounceMs=120\n 220ms flush <redacted-group-1> items=2 debounceMs=180\n# exit code 0\n```\n\nThis shows the changed batching behavior with real WhatsApp conversation identifiers redacted: the `debounceMs: 0` direct override flushes two messages immediately as separate items, while the other direct conversation and the group conversation batch two quick messages into one flush using their own per-conversation windows.\n\nFollow-up doc validation after aligning account/root precedence text:\n\n```text\n$ pnpm docs:list\n# exit code 0\n```\n\n```text\n$ git diff --check\n# exit code 0\n```\n\nFocused validation run locally from the PR branch:\n\n```text\n$ pnpm tsgo:test:extensions\n$ node scripts/run-tsgo.mjs -p test/tsconfig/tsconfig.extensions.test.json --incremental --tsBuildInfoFile .artifacts/tsgo-cache/extensions-test.tsbuildinfo\n# exit code 0\n```\n\n```text\n$ pnpm config:channels:check\n$ node --import tsx scripts/generate-bundled-channel-config-metadata.ts --check\n# exit code 0\n```\n\n```text\n$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run --config test/vitest/vitest.runtime-config.config.ts src/config/zod-schema.providers-whatsapp.test.ts\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\nTests       11 passed (11)\n```\n\n```text\n$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run --config test/vitest/vitest.e2e.config.ts extensions/whatsapp/src/auto-reply.web-auto-reply.connection-and-logging.e2e.test.ts\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\nTests       29 passed (29)\n```\n\nThe e2e coverage verifies the runtime behavior that direct and group messages resolve different per-conversation debounce windows, including `0` for immediate processing and wildcard fallback for other conversations.\n",
